<p>Each model reduced to a handful of numbers. <strong>On disk</strong> is the
download; <strong>fits 8 / 16 GB</strong> allows 4k of context and ~1 GB of
runtime overhead. The hardware columns are the measured range across this
benchmark's machines of that class: generation speed on a fresh context, and
under it the time to read a 2k-token prompt. A single number is a class with
one measured machine so far, not a tighter estimate. A lane only anchors a
range if the calibrated cost model can describe it — one whose fit fails
that bar is a broken stack, not a slow machine, and is footnoted instead of
averaged in.</p>
<p class="muted">
{%- for c in class_cols %}<strong>{{ c.label }}</strong>: {{ c.members }}{% if not loop.last %} &nbsp;·&nbsp; {% endif %}{% endfor -%}
</p>

<table>
  <thead><tr><th>model</th><th>on disk</th><th>fits 8 GB</th><th>fits 16 GB</th>
  {% for c in class_cols %}<th>{{ c.label }}</th>{% endfor %}</tr></thead>
  <tbody>
  {% for m in models %}
    <tr><td><strong>{{ m.model }}</strong> <span class="muted">{{ m.quant }}</span>
        {%- if m.caveats %} <span class="caveatmark" title="{{ m.caveats }}">†</span>{% endif %}</td>
        <td>{{ "%.1f"|format(m.file_gb) }} GB</td>
        <td>{% if m.fits8 %}<span class="yes">✓</span>{% else %}<span class="no">✗</span>{% endif %}</td>
        <td>{% if m.fits16 %}<span class="yes">✓</span>{% else %}<span class="no">✗</span>{% endif %}</td>
        {% for c in class_cols %}{% set r = m.ranges[c.key] %}
        <td><strong>{{ r.fresh }}</strong> <span class="unit">tok/s</span>
            <span class="sub">2k prompt {{ r.t2k }}{% if r.t2k != "—" %} s{% endif %}</span></td>
        {% endfor %}</tr>
  {% endfor %}
  </tbody>
</table>
{% if models | selectattr("caveats") | list %}
<p class="muted">† {% for m in models if m.caveats %}{{ m.model }} — {{ m.caveats }}{% if not loop.last %}; {% endif %}{% endfor %}</p>
{% endif %}
{% if rejects %}
<p class="muted">Excluded from the ranges: {% for r in rejects %}{{ r }}{% if not loop.last %}; {% endif %}{% endfor %}.
Those measurements remain in the per-lane detail below and under
<strong>Evidence</strong>.</p>
{% endif %}
<p class="muted">{{ verdict }}{{ agreement }} The measurements behind every
number — machines, hardware ceilings, full cost curves — are under
<strong>Evidence</strong>.</p>

<details>
  <summary>per-lane detail — the numbers behind the ranges, one row per
  machine · provider</summary>
  <p><strong>Streamed per token</strong> is the weight bytes a single decoded
  token reads — with the KV growth it sets generation speed on any machine
  (generation is memory-bound: tokens/s ≈ effective bandwidth ÷ bytes per
  token). <strong>2k / 8k prompt</strong> are read off each lane's sweep curve;
  <strong>fresh / deep</strong> is generation on an empty vs filled context.
  "—" means the sweep's budget ended before that depth on that lane. Each lane
  keeps its dot's colour in every chart under <strong>Evidence</strong>.</p>
  <div class="cards">
  {% for m in models %}
    <div class="card">
      <h3>{{ m.model }} <span class="quant">{{ m.quant }}</span></h3>
      <p class="costs">{{ "%.1f"|format(m.file_gb) }} GB on disk ·
        {{ "%.1f"|format(m.body_gb) }} GB streamed/token ·
        {{ "%.0f"|format(m.kv_mb_per_1k) }} MB KV/1k tokens{% if m.kv_state_mb >= 1 %} + {{ "%.0f"|format(m.kv_state_mb) }} MB state{% endif %}</p>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>lane</th><th>class</th><th>2k prompt</th><th>8k prompt</th>
        <th>fresh tok/s</th><th>deep tok/s</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
        {% for a in m.anchors %}
          <tr><td><i class="dot" style="--c:var(--{{ a.slot }})"></i>{{ a.lane }}</td>
              <td class="muted">{{ a.cls }}{% if a.rejected %} · not in ranges{% endif %}</td>
              <td>{{ a.t2k }}</td>
              <td>{{ a.t8k }}</td>
              <td>{{ a.fresh }}</td>
              <td>{{ a.deep }}</td></tr>
        {% endfor %}
        </tbody>
      </table>
      {% if m.caveats %}<p class="caveat">{{ m.caveats }}</p>{% endif %}
    </div>
  {% endfor %}
  </div>
</details>
